{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-79189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/assets/20c1f81e9737856b7cf1fbf031fd42988a37eb418303114d95f6521c0401fa61.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(4,-3)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(6,-3)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,-3).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2e8231d139bde46a09ff8096cf4b741c1a62a00fff4504bc2acf87abe788f94f"} {"question" "q-79189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3"] ["math" "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4"] ["math" "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3"] ["math" "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3"] ["math" "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =49({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =49(1) \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2e8231d139bde46a09ff8096cf4b741c1a62a00fff4504bc2acf87abe788f94f"} {"question" "q-79219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=14"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=7"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=56"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{7}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79219" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2e8231d139bde46a09ff8096cf4b741c1a62a00fff4504bc2acf87abe788f94f"} {"question" "q-79220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=2"] ["math" "(x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=4"] ["math" "(x-1)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=2"] ["math" "(x-1)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=4"] ["math" "(x-1)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-79220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-106049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/assets/2135f70f6703878d5f82d3524c890af08e194d7fdfbe92e97a5305d142f73779.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(2,1)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-106049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(2,1).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-106049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2e8231d139bde46a09ff8096cf4b741c1a62a00fff4504bc2acf87abe788f94f"} {"question" "q-106049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-106049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2"] ["math" "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1"] ["math" "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1"] ["math" "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2"] ["math" "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-106049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-321976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/assets/fbe6faf7f14f9ce51c299f18f9596c08c650079d2ca23398180baff8ea965553.png)\n\nThe three-quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-321976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a three-quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/assets/28d5eaee4de3a6100a92fa017b71d9860ea19cd7073284b41511dda4155ecb6d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the required three-quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the three-quarter circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-321976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2e8231d139bde46a09ff8096cf4b741c1a62a00fff4504bc2acf87abe788f94f"} {"question" "q-321976" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-321976" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-321976" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"} {"question" "q-321976" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/state.json" "file_sha256" "e09aeefdc0bd52c2beab4a3c07c46da9c2c65a7c831e84f35cd074720797d473"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1158 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
